早上8点吃早餐。然后我们清理战壕，检查并擦拭我们的步枪。我们整个上午都在工作。他所说的工作是指，我们在大部分时间里都在战壕上工作。我们挖土，加固胸墙。我们，你知道的，试图摆除一些害虫。我们做所有这些类似的事情。然后到12点，我们吃午饭。我们从1点到6点继续工作。所以他们基本上就像是一群筑路工什么的。然后我们，接着士兵们再次进食。我们在黄昏时分进行约一小时的“警戒”（Stand Two）。我们通宵工作，然后在黎明前进行一小时的“警戒”，这就是总体计划。听起来很有趣。这并非世界末日，对吧？我的意思是，总比在煤矿里强吧？好吧，正如我们所讨论的，格雷夫斯睡了一小会儿，然后在凌晨1点，邓恩叫醒了他，说：“我带你参观一下”。于是他们非常小心翼翼地探头越过胸墙，格雷夫斯第一次瞥见了无人区。天很黑，他几乎什么也看不见，但过了一会儿天亮一点时，他拿了一个潜望镜看了看。他可以看到德国战壕，那是用一排沙袋标记出来的，距离只有400 yards。虽然他在回忆录里没有真正提到这一点，但这一定是一个

49
00:23:26.000 --> 00:24:06.000
marmalade, so not bad, and just as they're getting stuck in, Dunn's manservant, his batman, his man called Kingdom, rushes in, his eyes blank with hot. horror and excitement and he says gas, sir, gas, they're using gas. And Dunn says the most British thing that anybody's ever said. He says, very well, Kingdom, bring me my respirator from the other room and another pot of marmalade. Can you, can you eat marmalade while you're wearing a gas mask? I think marmalade sure is the priority, no? You'd smear it all over your tube, wouldn't you? If you want to find out what happened next, I think the thing to do is to read the book, because I don't want to do the whole book. But I thought we'd
令人毛骨悚然的时刻。经历了这段旅程，经历了数月的等待，你终于看到了敌人的防线。只有400码远。你知道，你几分钟内就能走到那里，虽然你会被射杀。而且无人区目前还不是那种翻滚的烂泥，对吧？还能看到花朵。不，他说这是一片平坦的草地，长着矢车菊之类的植物，长草，几个弹坑，有一架飞机的残骸，他能看到铁丝网，他们的和我们的，但仅此而已。他又打了个盹，听着士兵们抱怨他们的虱子，这对他们来说是个大问题，我们等会儿会回到这个话题，然后他们在掩体里吃早餐，有培根、鸡蛋、咖啡、吐司和

50
00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:35.000
would maybe talk about some of the things that have come up in Graves' accounts so far. So first of all, the trenches. So the trenches, as you said, Tom, they are relatively new. You know, when Graves arrives, they are only months old. They were improvised. They were cut into the clay and the chalk in a hurry. And a key thing for people to remember, the Allies, when they dug their trenches, did not think, well, we're going to be here four years, so we better make them good.
橘子果酱，所以还不错。就在他们大快朵颐时，邓恩的贴身男仆，他的勤务兵，一个叫金（Kingdom）的人冲了进来，他的眼睛里充满了惊恐和兴奋，他说：“有毒气，长官，有毒气，他们在用毒气。”邓恩说了一句任何人说过的最英国化的话。他说：“很好，金，把我在另一个房间的呼吸器拿来，再拿一罐果酱。”你能在戴着防毒面具的同时吃果酱吗？我想果酱肯定是首要任务，对吧？你会把它抹得到处都是，不是吗？如果你想知道接下来发生了什么，我想最好的办法是去读这本书，因为我不想把整本书都讲完。但我认为我们

51
00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:57.000
they actually thought we might be here for a couple of weeks and then we're going to hopefully resume the offensive and drive the Germans further back east. And are they still thinking that at this point? I think by now, so we're in May 1915, there probably is an awareness that this is, this is why they're working so hard on the trenches that they're going to be here for a little bit longer.
也许可以谈谈格雷夫斯目前的叙述中出现的一些事情。首先，关于战壕。所以正如你所说，汤姆，这些战壕是相对较新的。你知道，当格雷夫斯到达时，它们只有几个月大。它们是临时修建的。它们是在仓促之中在粘土和白垩土中挖出来的。对人们来说，一个关键的事情要记住，盟军在挖战壕时，并没有想过，‘好吧，我们要在这里待四年，所以我们最好把它修好。’

52
00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:21.000
So the way the trenches work, you've got these kind of barricades and parapets that are built really of earth, of soil and of rubble, and people would reinforce them with wooden planks and with sandbags and then they would lie in the ground of the trench with these wooden planks, these duckboards. And they gave them names. They would often name after London Street, so Oxford Street, Regent Street and whatnot. And an intersection would be named after a famous junction in London.
他们实际上认为我们可能在这里待上一两周，然后希望恢复攻势，把德国人进一步赶向东方。他们在这个时候还在这么想吗？我想现在，也就是在1915年5月，可能已经有一种意识，这就是为什么他们在战壕上工作得如此辛苦，他们要在这里多待一会儿。

53
00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:38.000
So Hyde Park Corner, Oxford Circus and so on. Elephant and Castle. Yeah, I mean, I suppose, conceivably. Probably not. Probably not. And they would also make up names for the German lines opposite them. So there's a bit in Siegfried Sassoon where Siegfried Sassoon says, Our objective was pint trench,
所以战壕的运作方式是，你有这些由泥土、土壤和瓦砾真正建造的类似路障和胸墙的东西，人们用木板和沙袋加固它们，然后他们会在战壕的底部铺上这些木板，即所谓的“防潮木板”（duckboards）。他们给它们起了名字。他们经常以伦敦的街道来命名，比如牛津街、摄政街之类。一个交叉口会以伦敦著名的交通枢纽命名。

54
00:25:38.000 --> 00:26:05.000
taking bitter and beer and clearing ale and vat and also Pils and Lane. So they're name them all, the German trenches all after beers. Now on the German trenches, everybody agrees the German trenches are much better. They're deeper, they are safer, they're better defended, they're more comfortable. And the reason for that, obviously, is the Germans are there for the long haul. They're like what we have we hold. So it's not just about the German. being better at building things.
比如海德公园角、牛津圆环等等。象堡（Elephant and Castle）。是的，我的意思是，我想，这完全可以想象。可能没有吧。可能没有。他们还会为他们对面的德国战线起名字。萨松（Siegfried Sassoon）的作品中有一段，萨松说，我们的目标是“品脱战壕”（pint trench），

55
00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:22.000
Maybe they are better at building things, I don't know. I mean, they use some reinforced concrete, don't they? Which seems a kind of typical Hun trick. Well, when people do take German trenches, they're stunned. How good they are. They have kind of ceiling beams. They have nice sort of cladded walls. Cinemas.
拿下“苦啤酒”（bitter）、“生啤酒”（beer）、“清淡艾尔”（clearing ale）、“大桶”（vat）还有“比尔森”（Pils）和“巷道”（Lane）。所以他们把所有的德国战壕都用啤酒来命名。现在关于德国战壕，大家都承认德国战壕要好得多。它们更深，更安全，防守更好，更舒适。显然，其中的原因在于德国人要在那里进行长期作战。他们就像是“我们拥有我们所占领的”。所以这不仅仅是关于德国人更擅长建造东西。

56
00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:43.000
In the dugouts, they have kind of skylights and alcoves, sort of decorative alcoves and things. And they go really deep, don't they? I mean, some of them go so deep that actually they're kind of impervious to to anything the British can fire at them? Well, this is one of the issues when the British think, oh, we'll just clear the German trenches with artillery bombardment. They don't realize just how deep-seated these German trenches are.
也许他们更擅长建造东西，我不知道。我的意思是，他们使用了一些钢筋混凝土，对吧？这似乎是一种典型的德国鬼子把戏。好吧，当人们确实占领德国战壕时，他们都惊呆了。它们太棒了。它们有某种天花板横梁。它们有漂亮的镶板墙。有电影院。

57
00:26:43.000 --> 00:27:05.000
Graves talks, the one thing he obviously talks about in the trench, which is the most common thing that people associate with them, is mud. And this is the aspect of trench life that soldiers mention the most in subsequent accounts. So there's a diary of the war by a guy called Captain Alexander Stewart, which was published in 2009. He joined the Scottish Rifles in 19. He says of the mud. Mud is a bad description.
在掩体里，它们有天窗和壁龛，某种装饰性的壁龛之类的东西。它们挖得真的很深，对吧？我的意思是，其中一些深到实际上可以免疫英国人能发射的任何武器？好吧，这就是问题之一，当英国人认为，哦，我们只要用炮击清除德国战壕就行了。他们没有意识到这些德国战壕挖得有多深。

58
00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:21.000
It's not mud. The soil was more like a thick slime. When walking one sank several inches, it was difficult to withdraw the feet. The consequence was that men who were standing still or sitting down got embedded in the slime and were unable to extricate themselves. And as the trenches were so shallow,
格雷夫斯谈到了他在战壕里明显谈到的一件事，这是人们将战壕联系得最多的一件事，那就是泥土。这是士兵在随后的叙述中提及最多的战壕生活的一个方面。所以有一个叫亚历山大·斯图尔特（Alexander Stewart）上尉的人写的战争日记，于2009年出版。他于1919年加入了苏格兰步兵团。他是这样形容泥土的：“泥土”是一个糟糕的描述。

59
00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:36.000
they had to stay where they were all day. God, imagine being stuck there. But here's the thing. You stuck there. And if it's in the middle of a battle, you're a sitting duck. And there are a lot of a counter. actually, reading through soldiers' accounts, there's loads of them where they say
它不是泥土。土壤更像是一种浓稠的黏液。走的时候会陷下去好几英寸，很难把脚拔出来。结果就是，那些站着不动或坐着的人被嵌入了黏液中，无法自拔。由于战壕太浅，

60
