63
00:28:47.000 --> 00:28:58.000
this is a French stretcher bearer called Raymond Clemell. He said, without telling anybody, I take my clothes off and I see hundreds of lice and lava jumping out at me. They're everywhere in my shirt, my trousers, my underwear, etc.
这是一个名叫雷蒙德·克勒梅尔的法国担架兵说的。他说，在不告诉任何人的情况下，我脱下衣服，看到数百只虱子和幼虫跳出来。我的衬衫、裤子、内衣等到处都是。

64
00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:23.000
I shake my clothes as much as I can. and I finally wear them again. The only choice we have is to wait for the next relief and then to boil our clothes. So is this a regular occurrence that people are taking their clothes off and shaking the lice out, do you think? Yeah, I think it is. I mean, you're, there's no sort of personal discretion, as it were. There's not much place for that on the Western Front, really. You're going to the toilet altogether on a kind of plank and whatnot.
我尽全力抖落衣服上的脏东西，最后还是得把它们穿上。我们唯一的选择就是等待下一批换防的人来，然后把衣服煮沸。所以你认为大家脱下衣服抖掉虱子是常态吗？是的，我认为是的。我的意思是，根本没有什么所谓的个人隐私可言。在西线战场上，基本上没有这种地方。大家都是在一块木板上一起上厕所之类的。

65
00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:52.000
And you'd rather be naked than bitten to death by fleas? Of course. And the lice carry something called trench fever. And trench fever is an infectious disease. It's extremely common in the trenches, and the worst cases can be fatal. So J.R. Tolkien was invalided out at the Somme with a very severe case of trench fever. And ironically, the trench fever probably saved his life because if he'd stayed in the Somme, he could easily have died. A.A. Milne had trench fever. C.S. Lewis had trench fever.
那你宁愿赤身裸体，也不愿被跳蚤咬死？当然了。而且虱子会传播一种叫作战壕热的疾病。战壕热是一种传染病。在战壕里极为常见，最严重的情况可能是致命的。因此，J.R.R.托尔金在索姆河战役中因患上严重的战壕热而被遣送回国。具有讽刺意味的是，战壕热很可能救了他的命，因为如果他继续留在索姆河，他很可能会丧命。A.A.米恩得过战壕热。C.S.刘易斯得过战壕热。

66
00:29:52.000 --> 00:30:28.000
God, it's hard to think of A.A. Milne, isn't it, with trench fever? Winnie the poo with lice. He probably would have had fleas, certainly. Well, what about Winnie the Pooh with rats? So Graves has a section in his memoir where he's talking about rats, and he says there was a new officer who turned up. And his first night, he goes to bed, and he wakes and he hears this scuffle on his bed. There were two rats on his blankets tussling for the possession of a severed hand. This was thought a great joke, says Graves. And again, the rats are so common they are ubiquitous.
天哪，很难把A.A.米恩和战壕热联系在一起，对吧？长着虱子的小熊维尼。他肯定也得过跳蚤。那么，长着老鼠的小熊维尼呢？所以格里夫斯在他的回忆录里写到老鼠时说，曾有一个新军官来到这里。第一天晚上，他上床睡觉，醒来时听到床上有什么东西在争夺。他的毯子上有两只老鼠在为一个断手争夺不休。格里夫斯说，这被认为是一个天大的笑话。同样，老鼠也是如此普遍，随处可见。

67
00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:45.000
So like the flies, they're drawn by the dead body. is they become huge. Because they're eating dead soldiers. Yeah. There are so many accounts of them being as big as cats. And often the men deliberately do not kill them because they will just be left there to putrify and they will stink and spread disease.
所以就像苍蝇一样，它们被尸体吸引，而且变得体型巨大。因为它们在吃死去的士兵。是的。有无数的记录说它们长得像猫一样大。而且士兵们通常故意不杀它们，因为如果把它们留在那里腐烂，它们会发出恶臭并传播疾病。

68
00:30:45.000 --> 00:31:12.000
Or the dead bodice will draw other rats to kind of feed on them. So this is another Tommy writing home. In the night we have heaps of company rats and mice and the other livestock. Every time you wake, the rats are fighting and squeaking all over you. The other night, one took a flying jump onto my face. There's a famous poem by Ivan Rosenberg called Break of Day in the Trenches, which kind of almost implies a fondness for what he calls a queer sardonic rat.
或者说死去的尸体会吸引更多的老鼠来觅食。所以这是另一个写信回家的汤米（英国士兵的昵称）。在夜里，我们有成堆的老鼠、小鼠和其他伴侣。每次你醒来，老鼠都在你身上打架、吱吱叫。前天晚上，有一只老鼠猛地跳到了我的脸上。伊万·罗森伯格有一首著名的诗叫《战壕里的破晓》，几乎暗示了他对一种他称之为古怪而冷嘲热讽的老鼠的喜爱。

69
00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:44.000
And he imagines the rat brushing up against his hand and then going and brushing up against a German hand. And say, droll rat, they would shoot you if they knew your cosmopolitan sympathies. So it may be that in, you know, a bit like Christopher Robin befriending, piglet. If you're in the trench, you might befriend it. My friend a rat. I mean, I think that... Well, you might, Tom. I think I draw the line of preferring a rat, frankly. I take friendship where I can get it. That's fair. All this sounds pretty nightmarish. And some listeners are thinking
他想象着老鼠擦过他的手，然后又去擦过一只德国人的手。并且说，滑稽的老鼠啊，如果他们知道你对全人类都有同情心，他们就会开枪打死你。所以也许，这有点像克里斯托弗·罗宾和皮杰交朋友。如果你在战壕里，你可能会和它交朋友。和一只老鼠交朋友。我的意思是，我认为……好吧，你可能会的，汤姆。坦白说，我觉得我绝不会更喜欢一只老鼠。我有机会就会交朋友。这倒也是。这一切听起来都像是一场噩梦。有些听众可能会想

70
00:31:44.000 --> 00:32:11.000
this doesn't sound like a great laugh. So you are muddy, you're wet, you're cold, you've got rats. There's also the shells going off all around you. And it's also very boring. There's a lot of waiting around. So there's a young German writer called Ernst Junger, who we will feature more after the break. And Junger arrived very excited and very idealistic. Can't couldn't wait to get, have a crack at the British. And he said in his memoir, Storm of Steel, after only short time of the regiment, we'd become thoroughly disillusioned.
这听起来可一点都不好笑又好玩。所以你满身泥泞，又湿又冷，还冷得发抖，到处都是老鼠。周围还有炮弹在爆炸。而且也非常无聊。有大量的时间都在等待。所以有一个年轻的德国作家叫恩斯特·容格，我们将在中场休息后详细介绍他。容格到达时非常兴奋，充满了理想主义。迫不及待地想去跟英国人较量一番。他在回忆录《钢铁风暴》中说，在部队待了很短一段时间后，我们就彻底幻灭了。

71
00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:34.000
Instead of the danger that we had hoped for, we'd be given dirt, work and sleepless nights. Worse still was the boredom, which was still more innovating for the soldier than the proximity of death. But one point to make as we approach the break. It's really important to say, you said earlier, it's a lot better than working down a mine. And I think you're right. And a lot of soldiers enjoy it.
没有我们所期望的危险，我们得到的只有泥土、繁重的劳务和不眠之夜。更糟糕的是无聊，对于士兵来说，这种无聊比死神临头的威胁更令人筋疲力尽。但在我们接近中场休息时，有一点需要指出。非常重要的一点是，你刚才说，这比在矿井里工作好得多。我想你是对的。而且很多士兵其实很享受这种生活。

